            "request_id": "c1d055e3-8793-4ed1-90f9-13e7c0e79412",
            "answer": "**MLflow tracing for LangGraph agents**\n\nWhen you create a LangGraph agent with the **Agent\u202fBricks CLI**, tracing is enabled automatically. The CLI creates an MLflow experiment under  \n\n```\n/Shared/agentbricks_traces/<project\u2011or\u2011agent\u2011name>\n```\n\nand every time the agent runs (whether locally with `agentbricks dev` or after deployment with `agentbricks deploy`) the execution trace is logged to that experiment.  \n\n**What gets traced**\n\n| Item | Description |\n|------|-------------|\n| **Agent run metadata** \u2013 start/end timestamps, duration, status, and the agent\u2019s configuration. |\n| **Tool invocations** \u2013 each bound tool (e.g., web\u2011search, Genie, sandbox, Unity\u202fCatalog functions) is recorded as a separate step, showing the input parameters, the tool\u2019s response, and any downstream errors. |\n| **Memory & session store interactions** \u2013 reads/writes to the managed memory or session stores are captured, giving you a full view of state changes across a conversation. |\n| **Model calls** \u2013 when the agent calls a model (via Databricks Model Serving), the request/response payloads are logged as part of the trace. |\n\nThese traces appear as **MLflow runs** inside the experiment, and you can explore them in the MLflow UI (or via the MLflow Python API) to debug, audit, or analyze agent behavior.\n\n**Typical workflow**\n\n```bash\n# 1. Create a LangGraph agent (tracing is on by default)\nagentbricks init langgraph my-agent\n\n# 2. Run locally \u2013 traces are sent to the default experiment\nagentbricks dev   # \u2192 http://localhost:8000\n\n# 3. List the collected traces\nagentbricks tracing list\n\n# 4. (Optional) Bind the agent to a specific existing experiment\nagentbricks tracing bind --experiment-id 12345\n\n# 5. Turn tracing off completely\nagentbricks tracing unbind\n```\n\n**How it integrates with tools**\n\nWhen you add tool bindings in `agent.toml` (e.g., a web\u2011search MCP, a Genie agent, or a Unity\u202fCatalog function), each tool call is automatically wrapped by the tracing layer. The trace records:\n\n* `tool_id` and `source` (MCP, Genie, sandbox, UC function, etc.)\n* The **auth mode** (`user` or `app`) used for the call\n* Input payload sent to the tool\n* Output returned (or error details)\n\nBecause the tracing data lives in MLflow, you can query it alongside other experiment metrics, compare runs across different tool configurations, and even visualize tool\u2011level latency.\n\n**Key points**\n\n* Tracing is **on by default** for LangGraph agents created with `agentbricks init`.  \n* The default experiment path is `/Shared/agentbricks_traces/<agent\u2011name>`.  \n* You can re\u2011bind to any existing MLflow experiment or disable tracing entirely with `agentbricks tracing bind` / `agentbricks tracing unbind`.  \n* All tool invocations, memory/session store accesses, and model calls are captured as part of the run, giving end\u2011to\u2011end observability.\n\n**Sources**\n\n* \u201cTracing is on by default\u2026 binds a default `/Shared/agentbricks_traces/<project>` MLflow experiment\u201d \u2013\u202f[Agent Bricks CLI \u2013 Tracing overview](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/agent-bricks-cli)  \n* \u201cTo list traces\u2026 `agentbricks tracing list`; to bind a specific MLflow experiment\u2026 `agentbricks tracing bind --experiment-id <experiment-id>`; to turn tracing off\u2026 `agentbricks tracing unbind`\u201d \u2013\u202f[Agent Bricks CLI \u2013 Tracing commands](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/agent-bricks-cli)  ",
